idx":9,"text":"165"}]},"footnotes":[],"label":"table","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":142.159912109375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":107.7899169921875,"r":501.4753112792969,"t":350.875244140625},"charspan":[0,0],"page_no":1}],"references":[],"self_ref":"#/tables/0"}],"texts":[{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"FIG. 1l: Our Replica Electromagnet","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":755.5566813151041,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":215.66666666666666,"r":394.3333333333333,"t":769.5566813151041},"charspan":[0,34],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/0","text":"FIG. 1l: Our Replica Electromagnet"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"To confirm some of the suggestions made about Henry's experiments; we constructed a replica of Henry's Albany magnet, shown in Figure 11 above Each coil was in the same way as experiments 8-16. There are a couple of main differences between our magnet and Henry's: 1) Our magnet was forged from mild steel, which has a higher carbon content than wrought iron; giving it some residual magnetism after the external field is removed. Mild steel also takes much longer to reach saturation; and has a smaller initial permeability; 2) We used 9, 60 ft strands of high purity enameled copper wire; which has a much thinner insulation than Henry's silk, allowing for two differences; especially a different magnetic material; can be shown to have a large effect on our results. Nevertheless; all the data collected below should be consistent enough to establish certain comparisons with Henry's results. wound using","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":518.5566813151042,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.0,"r":540.3333333333334,"t":736.2233479817709},"charspan":[0,907],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"To confirm some of the suggestions made about Henry's experiments; we constructed a replica of Henry's Albany magnet, shown in Figure 11 above Each coil was in the same way as experiments 8-16. There are a couple of main differences between our magnet and Henry's: 1) Our magnet was forged from mild steel, which has a higher carbon content than wrought iron; giving it some residual magnetism after the external field is removed. Mild steel also takes much longer to reach saturation; and has a smaller initial permeability; 2) We used 9, 60 ft strands of high purity enameled copper wire; which has a much thinner insulation than Henry's silk, allowing for two differences; especially a different magnetic material; can be shown to have a large effect on our results. Nevertheless; all the data collected below should be consistent enough to establish certain comparisons with Henry's results. wound using"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"When measuring the B-field at different  areas on the poles; it was observed that the B-Field increased when the probe was brought towards the inner edge of the Furthermore, the B-field was at maximum at the inner corners of each (about double that of the center) . Even though the reading was higher at the corners; it was difficult to consistent measurements there, so these results were discarded. pole. pole get","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":418.8900146484375,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.0,"r":540.0,"t":512.8900146484375},"charspan":[0,415],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"When measuring the B-field at different  areas on the poles; it was observed that the B-Field increased when the probe was brought towards the inner edge of the Furthermore, the B-field was at maximum at the inner corners of each (about double that of the center) . Even though the reading was higher at the corners; it was difficult to consistent measurements there, so these results were discarded. pole. pole get"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"To test the idea of flux leakage though a magnetic circuit; each coil was attached to a constant current of 5 and the B-Field was measured at the center of each pole. The results are shown below in Table I. Amps","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":358.2233479817708,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":70.66666666666667,"r":539.6666666666666,"t":412.2233479817708},"charspan":[0,211],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"To test the idea of flux leakage though a magnetic circuit; each coil was attached to a constant current of 5 and the B-Field was measured at the center of each pole. The results are shown below in Table I. Amps"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"TABLE F: B- Field measurements taken for each coil supplied with a constant current","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":128.55668131510413,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":99.0,"r":511.6666666666667,"t":143.22334798177087},"charspan":[0,83],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":"TABLE F: B- Field measurements taken for each coil supplied with a constant current"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"23","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":74.55668131510413,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":293.3333333333333,"r":306.6666666666667,"t":85.22334798177087},"charspan":[0,2],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"23"}],"version":"1.10.0"}